{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-67785",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237869/assets/da7cc00005fbeb686443e2075009fda7c68f6d379a8ddb924a7dfe5b60e6ab16.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈38.637,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{67.2}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{67.2}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈27.656,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =38.637+27.656 \\\\  & =66.293\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $66.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
